WEBVTT

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:46.600
The polar regions are more hostile to life than any other part of the Earth.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.360
Human beings have little natural protection against the cold.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:59.960
So why, for thousands of years, have we endured the hardships that come from living here?

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:22.680
And what keeps us coming back today to the farthest extremes of our planet?

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:29.000
Winter in the most northerly town on Earth, Longyearbyen.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.520
There for three months of the year the sun never rises.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:55.200
Only the full moon, which never sets as far north as this, sheds any light into the darkness.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:01.680
This town was built here in Svalbard, only 700 miles from the North Pole, to support

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.320
a mine.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:14.440
The Arctic is rich in coal, oil and minerals.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:25.160
In Siberia, the Russian Arctic, the mineral wealth has given rise to large cities.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:32.840
This is Norilsk, the coldest city on Earth.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:38.240
Temperatures regularly drop to below minus 50 degrees centigrade.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:50.240
Fuel freezes in the tanks of the trucks and has to be melted in a rather alarming way.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.400
Ropes are frozen into the rivers for nine months of the year.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.600
The ice must be cut away from their hulls because steel becomes brittle and vulnerable

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.320
to the thickening ice.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:12.600
Like all Arctic cities, Norilsk depends on a power plant, which heats everybody's home.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:18.000
Waste heat from the plant even keeps a lake ice free all winter.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:31.920
Even at air temperatures of minus 50, the Norilsk Walrus Club come here every day.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:44.800
There is evidence that a dip in cold water is good for the immune system, but when the

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.760
water is a degree above freezing, it's hard to imagine that the benefits could outweigh

00:03:49.760 --> 00:04:04.920
the pain.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:12.800
Today's Arctic city dwellers can lead an almost normal existence thanks to technology.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:19.960
But towns and cities are very recent arrivals in the polar landscape.

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:24.720
The polar regions are the least populated part of our planet.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:32.240
Most of the Arctic remains empty of human beings.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:38.960
In the north, the pole itself is covered by a freezing ocean.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:59.760
Inside it lie vast lands, of which Siberia is the largest and coldest.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:05.080
Human beings first ventured onto the Great Plains of Siberia, the tundra, thousands

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:17.680
of years ago, and some live here still.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.320
The Dolgan are one of the few tribes who still live in much the same way as those first Arctic

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:33.640
pioneers.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:36.720
They are reindeer people.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.680
Reindeer are one of the few animals that can endure these bitterly cold conditions, scraping

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:50.480
a living by nibbling tiny plants that survive beneath the snow.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:55.960
Nobody has ever totally tamed reindeer, but today's animals are docile enough to allow

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:18.960
the Dolgan to drive them across the tundra in an everlasting search for their food.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:36.160
This is a typical Dolgan village, home to just two extended families.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:41.200
Here in the coldest part of the Arctic, the only way to get water for nine months of the

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:50.360
year is to melt ice from the frozen rivers.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.760
At least there's no problem preventing food from decay.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:00.680
Outside is one big deep freeze.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.160
Survival is only possible because of reindeer fur.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.800
It makes wonderfully warm clothing, though small children still have to be sewn into

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:14.080
their clothes to prevent instant frostbite.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:21.180
The Dolgan even use reindeer fur to insulate their huts.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:26.160
This is living at its most communal.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.920
Good relations with the in-laws are essential.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:37.000
Reindeer are so valuable that the people only eat them if they have no other choice.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:43.800
Their favorite food is raw fish from the frozen rivers.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:50.640
Every week or so, these families have to travel to find new feeding grounds for their herds.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:57.080
First, they round up their strongest animals with lassoos, a skill that their ancestors

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:07.840
brought with them when they came north from Central Asia.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:27.720
And then, literally, they move house.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:31.120
The whole Dolgan village can move on in just a few hours.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:58.160
Over the year, they travel hundreds of miles like this across the vast tundra.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:03.080
It was the herds of reindeer wandering over the lands of the Arctic that brought the first

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:05.720
Dolgan here.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:08.880
Other people, however, took on an even greater challenge.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:16.160
They left the land and looked for their food out on the frozen sea.

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:29.680
Here, in the shifting world of the sea ice, they found sea mammals.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:56.480
This valley is called回來 and however for crushing nature, it is called literacy.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:08.680
Pasha leads a group of Inuit men in Chukotka, the north-eastern corner of Russia.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:13.040
The men have travelled many hours from home in the bitter cold, fighting their way through

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:19.320
a dangerous maze.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:39.960
These hundred-tonne ice floes could crush their small boats like eggshells.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:48.760
The men have big families, and this is the only way they have of feeding them.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:54.160
Pasha is looking for the puffs of steamy air produced by their quarry.

00:10:54.160 --> 00:11:03.440
An animal that is bigger than many Arctic whales.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.760
A two-tonne seal with formidable tusks.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:15.720
A walrus.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:22.320
It's heading for open water. They must reach it before it dives.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:36.040
An angry walrus could easily overturn the boats.

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:40.880
The harpoon sticks firmly in the walrus's thick layer of blubber, and floats attached

00:11:40.880 --> 00:12:00.720
to it to prevent the animal from diving.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:17.840
Pasha wants to kill quickly with a single clean shot.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:25.800
The hunters are exhausted after a long day, but they still have a lot more work to do.

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:36.440
This enormous prize will feed everyone's family for weeks.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.920
It will take many hours to butcher.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:49.000
Nothing will go to waste.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:59.360
The meat is parceled up in bags made of the animal's skin.

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:03.840
It's midnight, but the sun is still up.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:07.560
Summer is almost here.

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:15.040
This far north, the seasons change fast.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:38.680
The sun is rising higher and growing warmer with each passing day.

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:44.200
The transformation from winter to summer is so dramatic that it dominates the lives of

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:48.240
all who live here.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:54.080
The ice around the coast has almost disappeared, and gone are the seals and walrus that Pasha

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:58.640
and his men relied on.

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:07.000
They set off on another search for food.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:17.560
Their destination is an island in the bay.

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:22.680
Their walrus skin boat is an ancient design, light enough to carry high up the beach so

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:35.960
it doesn't drift away.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:41.760
Once again, the men will have to work as a team, but one of them will be taking very

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:48.200
serious risks.

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:52.960
The lightest man in the group, Kolia, is also the oldest.

00:14:52.960 --> 00:15:15.000
He will trust his life to a length of old nylon rope and the strength of his friends.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:21.520
These hundred-meter-high cliffs are home to thousands of gillimots, and Kolia is after

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:27.160
their eggs.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:34.240
He relies on the men above to lower him to the right place.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:45.360
Kolia is tough, but his stress is obvious.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:49.660
The men lower Kolia down to the bottom of the cliff, and from there he works his way

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:54.360
back up the crumbling rock face.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:16:22.440
This

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:28.840
mission will produce no more than about 50 eggs, but at least there's no need to carry

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:44.660
a packed lunch.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:50.640
Over the years, many men have fallen to their deaths collecting seabird eggs.

00:16:50.640 --> 00:17:04.640
This is truly dangerous work.

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:07.460
These Arctic peoples can't grow crops.

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:11.700
The frozen ground never thaws to allow them to do so.

00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:17.360
They rely on animals for their food, so the chance to collect a few dozen eggs has to

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:37.080
be taken, even if it means risking your life.

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:43.460
The change of season has transformed the Arctic's coastline, and in land the difference is just

00:17:43.460 --> 00:17:49.000
as extreme.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:58.900
July temperatures on the tundra can be surprisingly high, over 30 degrees centigrade.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:05.320
Reindeer now move not just to find fresh pasture, but also to avoid the summer swarms of bloodsucking

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:06.320
flies.

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:15.600
To keep their animals healthy, the local herders are driving them to the sea.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:21.400
The cooler conditions on the coast bring relief to the herds, and the chance every year for

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:24.600
different tribes to meet.

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:37.800
Pasha and his hunters live close by, they've had word of the herder's arrival.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:43.800
The hunter's cargo is highly prized for the winter ahead, fat-rich walrus meat that's

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:49.040
been fermenting in the skin bags for two months.

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:55.200
Today we'll see an exchange that has taken place every summer for centuries.

00:18:55.200 --> 00:19:00.040
The herders barter reindeer skins for walrus meat.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:09.440
Pooling their resources has helped these communities to survive for so long.

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:18.320
A fry-up of Gilimot eggs is all the better when shared with old friends.

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:22.640
Summer brings a brief chance for isolated peoples to meet.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:38.360
This is an opportunity to exchange news, arrange weddings, and tell the latest jokes.

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:41.440
By August, the summer is over.

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:46.600
Winter arrives only too swiftly, but the peoples of the Arctic, who came here originally to

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:52.280
hunt, have devised ways to deal with the hostile and changing conditions that have stood the

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:55.280
test of time.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:20:06.240
Today, there's a new draw to the Arctic.

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:24.440
This is Greenland, a territory of Denmark now known to be rich in oil and precious metals.

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:28.360
This sled team is part of the Danish Special Forces.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:35.000
They're on one of the world's toughest journeys, a 2,000-mile patrol, to maintain Denmark's

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:41.800
claim to this valuable wilderness.

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:54.720
But the patrol's mission is only possible with the help of man's oldest Arctic companion.

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:59.800
Rasmus and Roland have spent the summer months training and getting to know their team of

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:01.640
Greenland huskies.

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:17.720
They need to have a very close bond with every single dog.

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:22.640
The men are totally dependent on the stamina of their dogs, which will keep on running

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:31.880
all through the bitter cold of the winter.

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:40.680
This is the last time the team will see the sun for two months.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:46.480
The most intelligent dogs always lead, choosing the safest route, feeling for hidden crevasses

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:58.200
and thin ice.

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:04.200
This is one of six teams that patrol the whole of northeast Greenland, the only people in

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:12.840
an empty wilderness that is larger than France and Great Britain combined.

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:18.400
Mountains here are too extreme for current mining technology, but someday ways will be

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:25.840
found of digging out the huge mineral treasures that lie hidden within these mountains.

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:43.240
The patrol secures Denmark's claim to do so simply by being here.

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:48.000
But it's not the prospect of getting rich that makes men sign up for this patrol.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:52.080
It's a chance for the journey of a lifetime.

00:22:52.080 --> 00:23:00.360
The team travel over the ice for six months, covering up to 40 miles in a day.

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:06.760
Friendship and teamwork are essential if they're to succeed.

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:10.960
The dogs can sleep outside no matter how cold it gets.

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:18.560
Rasmus and Roland have a nice, cozy tent.

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:24.120
They have a few modern conveniences, including a radio with which they report their position

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:29.560
back to headquarters in Denmark and catch up on the latest news.

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:34.720
Right now it's the section of money.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:40.200
What's new in the economy in Denmark, the financial crisis and all the other things

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:44.120
that we actually don't care about out here.

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:50.200
If you can cope with the conditions, then winter in the Arctic can be magical, especially

00:23:50.200 --> 00:24:03.000
when the greatest light show on Earth is overhead.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:08.800
The first humans in the Arctic believed the Northern Lights, or Aurora Borealis, were

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:10.680
dancing spirits.

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:16.600
Now we know the lights are caused by electrically charged particles streaming from the sun,

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:24.640
attracted by the magnetic pull of the Earth's poles.

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:37.840
A big aurora storm contains enough energy to knock out satellite communications and

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:50.000
power supplies across the Northern Hemisphere, so understanding the aurora is vital.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:54.760
In Alaska, rockets are used to study the lights.

00:24:54.760 --> 00:25:01.960
A hundred miles up at the edge of outer space, the rockets release a cloud of glowing smoke

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:08.640
that's visible from Earth.

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:17.480
The smoke is blown by fierce winds which are generated by the aurora.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:22.640
Mapping the movement of the smoke helps scientists to understand how this unearthly spectacle

00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:45.680
affects our atmosphere.

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:51.280
They constantly monitor the aurora to help protect us from its effects, so the rest of

00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:57.000
us can simply enjoy the magic, just as the Arctic's first people must have done thousands

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:13.520
of years ago.

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:19.280
For all the many peoples of the Arctic, the aurora is a reminder of the sun's presence

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:26.280
throughout the dark days of winter.

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:30.920
But when the sun is below the horizon in the north, it's above it at the southern end of

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:35.200
our planet.

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:43.680
Here humanity's history has been very different.

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:50.880
Antarctica is far colder than the Arctic, and 99% of its land is permanently blanketed

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:53.560
by ice.

00:26:53.560 --> 00:27:03.200
Antarctica is so utterly remote and inhospitable that no people ever settled here.

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:17.160
It was only 200 years ago that the first human beings even glimpsed the vast continent.

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:22.160
The first people who crossed the southern ocean did so for the same reason that the

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:39.120
first people went to the Arctic Ocean to hunt sea mammals.

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:45.880
The populations of whales and seals are only now beginning to recover from 150 years of

00:27:45.880 --> 00:28:01.960
intensive hunting.

00:28:01.960 --> 00:28:07.400
But none of those hunters ever tried to venture into the frigid interior of the Antarctic

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:10.040
continent.

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:17.440
The first successful attempt to do that was made only 100 years ago.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:29.440
This hut was the base for one of the most famous expeditions in polar history.

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:35.680
It was from here in 1911 that Captain Scott and his team launched their attempt to be

00:28:35.680 --> 00:28:41.920
the first people to reach the South Pole.

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:48.320
The cold, dry conditions have preserved the interior of the hut almost exactly as the

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:55.960
expedition members left it.

00:28:55.960 --> 00:29:06.600
And photographer Herbert Ponting captured the spirit of the age of exploration.

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:12.120
These first explorers borrowed the techniques of the Arctic peoples.

00:29:12.120 --> 00:29:20.160
They wore fur gloves and boots and burned seal blubber to keep warm.

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:29.640
They built sleds based on a traditional Inuit design.

00:29:29.640 --> 00:29:39.760
They even made their sleeping bags from reindeer hide.

00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:46.640
Scott and his men sought the glory of discovery in an untouched wilderness and died in the

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:48.640
attempt.

00:29:48.640 --> 00:29:53.680
But he and those who followed him were the first to reveal the splendor of Antarctica

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:56.680
to the rest of the world.

00:29:56.680 --> 00:30:08.280
The lure of adventure still draws intrepid travelers today.

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:13.840
Like the first explorers, most modern visitors come during the brief summer when the cold

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:18.920
relents enough for the toughest icebreakers to reach the edge of the continent.

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:31.840
But most still need a helicopter to go further.

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:37.480
The scenery in Antarctica is magnificent and dramatic, but what really attracts people

00:30:37.480 --> 00:30:42.160
here is the wildlife.

00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:49.560
An emperor penguin colony is a particular highlight.

00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:54.040
Because human beings didn't arrive in the Antarctic until the past few centuries, the

00:30:54.040 --> 00:31:00.320
animals have never developed a fear of man.

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:07.480
But very strict regulations govern how close people can approach any wildlife.

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:14.480
And when visitors leave, they must take every trace of their visit away with them.

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:46.840
Since 1959, the whole of Antarctica has been protected by international treaty.

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:52.160
The nations of the world have agreed that no country can claim Antarctica or prospect

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:56.600
for its oil or minerals.

00:31:56.600 --> 00:32:01.800
The only significant human activities allowed here are those that extend our scientific

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:06.240
knowledge.

00:32:06.240 --> 00:32:29.520
But unlocking Antarctica's secrets requires some unusual tools.

00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:47.400
This brand new robot submarine has been designed to go far beyond the limits of any human.

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:55.760
Today this diver is putting the sub through its paces on one of its very first dives.

00:32:55.760 --> 00:33:02.000
It's designed to be small and nimble enough to explore the Antarctic seabed without damaging

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:17.960
it.

00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:23.280
The submarine's mission, as it journeys into the unknown, is to map the sea floor and look

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:30.280
for species new to science.

00:33:30.280 --> 00:33:45.800
The seawater here is a degree below zero, so even the toughest human diver can't stay

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:49.760
down for long.

00:33:49.760 --> 00:34:00.920
The submarine will explore deeper under the ice than anyone has gone before.

00:34:00.920 --> 00:34:05.520
From the depths of the ocean to the highest peaks of the land, new discoveries are being

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:23.080
made even in places which were first visited over a century ago.

00:34:23.080 --> 00:34:28.600
Mount Erebus was an irresistible draw to the legendary explorer Ernest Shackleton.

00:34:28.600 --> 00:34:34.600
In 1908 his men became the first to climb this active volcano.

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:40.640
They soon discovered that this is the coldest place on the Antarctic coast.

00:34:40.640 --> 00:34:45.280
Today's explorers still have to guard against frostbite in the height of summer when temperatures

00:34:45.280 --> 00:34:55.240
rarely creep above minus 30 degrees centigrade.

00:34:55.240 --> 00:35:10.200
The men had no idea of the extraordinary spectacle that lay beneath their feet.

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:16.440
Under the ice and snow is a network of caves which only a handful of expert cavers have

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:25.040
ever dared to enter.

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:34.400
This is the first scientific expedition to explore them in detail.

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:42.600
Here there are ice formations that occur nowhere else on Earth.

00:35:42.600 --> 00:36:06.480
Each cave contains its own unique collection of structures.

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:19.360
The team is mapping the caves to see how their shape changes over the years.

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:43.880
The steam leaking from vents in the side of the volcano is constantly sculpting this labyrinth

00:36:43.880 --> 00:36:48.360
that extends deep under the ice.

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:54.040
When the hot breath of the volcano hits the icy walls, the moisture in the air freezes

00:36:54.040 --> 00:37:09.700
into beautiful shapes.

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:15.140
Some of the crystals are so unusual that the cavers are investigating a remote but tantalizing

00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:20.200
possibility about their formation.

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:25.440
Could it be that some of these extraordinary crystal shapes are formed by highly specialized

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:43.680
bacteria living in the ice?

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:46.120
Nobody yet knows the answer.

00:37:46.120 --> 00:37:51.080
This is just one of the many strange mysteries that draw people to work in a place that is

00:37:51.080 --> 00:38:00.640
so hostile to human life.

00:38:00.640 --> 00:38:07.760
While some scientists come to Erebus to explore its bizarre ice caves, others visit the volcano

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:16.480
to study the innermost workings of our planet.

00:38:16.480 --> 00:38:22.720
Erebus is one of the most active volcanoes on Earth, but even so, volcanologists work

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:26.640
on the very rim of its crater.

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:32.520
They stand in the bitter cold, while 100 meters below them is a lava lake where temperatures

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:38.880
are over a thousand degrees centigrade.

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:59.280
This is a rare glimpse of the molten rock that lies beneath the Earth's crust.

00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:04.960
But research here is looking up as well as down, measuring how the gases that bubble

00:39:04.960 --> 00:39:13.160
out of the volcano influence the makeup of the air we breathe.

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:18.560
Antarctica is the best place to measure any changes in our atmosphere because it has the

00:39:18.560 --> 00:39:22.800
least polluted air on Earth.

00:39:22.800 --> 00:39:34.200
It's also the perfect place to launch more outward-looking missions.

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:39.600
This balloon, made of material no thicker than cling film, will eventually grow to be

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:44.720
300 meters tall.

00:39:44.720 --> 00:39:50.100
It will carry a device for detecting cosmic rays, tiny particles from the beginning of

00:39:50.100 --> 00:40:05.040
time that are only now reaching Earth.

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:10.900
The balloon will travel to the very edge of outer space to gather clues about the formation

00:40:10.900 --> 00:40:21.840
of the universe.

00:40:21.840 --> 00:40:27.760
Even today, very few ever make the journey inland from the coast.

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:34.320
We still know remarkably little about the interior of the continent.

00:40:34.320 --> 00:40:39.020
The people on this plane are trying to answer one of the fundamental questions.

00:40:39.020 --> 00:40:48.520
How much ice is there in Antarctica?

00:40:48.520 --> 00:40:57.000
They measure the depth of the ice sheet, which is over 4,000 meters in places, using radar.

00:40:57.000 --> 00:41:02.920
Their work will enable us to see how the volume of Antarctica's ice changes in the future.

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:20.960
It also makes it possible to map a hidden landscape.

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:26.400
This plane is following the same route through the Trans-Antarctic Mountains that Captain

00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:29.600
Scott took 100 years ago.

00:41:29.600 --> 00:41:42.400
His team hauled their sleds over 100 miles up this glacier, the Beardmoor.

00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:47.720
Skirting seemingly endless crevasses with no map to guide them and no idea of what lay

00:41:47.720 --> 00:42:02.440
ahead, it was a journey of extraordinary suffering.

00:42:02.440 --> 00:42:09.440
Their target lay beyond the mountains, over 3,000 meters above sea level, on the Antarctic

00:42:09.440 --> 00:42:16.840
Plateau.

00:42:16.840 --> 00:42:23.360
An unbroken sheet of ice larger than Western Europe, this is the coldest, the windiest,

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:30.000
the most lifeless place on Earth.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:36.080
Roald Amundsen's team narrowly defeated Scott's to become the first people to reach the South

00:42:36.080 --> 00:42:42.080
Pole on the 14th of December 1911.

00:42:42.080 --> 00:42:51.640
Nobody else successfully completed the journey for nearly 50 years after that.

00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:56.220
But since 1957, there's been a permanent base at the South Pole.

00:42:56.220 --> 00:43:02.040
You could even land a plane on the ice runway.

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:11.600
The early explorers would be astounded by the facilities at the South Pole today.

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:17.760
Working work isn't easy when the average summer temperature is minus 25 degrees centigrade.

00:43:17.760 --> 00:43:23.160
But despite the difficulties, the most high-tech scientific research station ever built was

00:43:23.160 --> 00:43:31.680
unveiled here in 2006.

00:43:31.680 --> 00:43:37.240
The brand new Amundsen-Scott South Pole station is designed to cope with the world's most

00:43:37.240 --> 00:43:40.080
extreme conditions.

00:43:40.080 --> 00:43:44.000
The building's sloping edge deflects the prevailing wind.

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:49.680
Beneath, there are stilts that can raise the whole building a further eight meters to keep

00:43:49.680 --> 00:44:01.120
it above the accumulating snow.

00:44:01.120 --> 00:44:06.880
Living inside is as close to being on a space station as you can find on Earth.

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:10.000
This space is totally self-sufficient.

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:14.720
The people are completely cut off from the outside world for more than half the year

00:44:14.720 --> 00:44:22.720
over winter.

00:44:22.720 --> 00:44:35.220
The total darkness makes this the perfect place to study the night sky.

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:40.800
The group of stargazers will be the most isolated community on our planet, but they will have

00:44:40.800 --> 00:44:48.200
all their needs catered for.

00:44:48.200 --> 00:44:54.400
There is even a greenhouse where fresh vegetables grow under artificial light all through the

00:44:54.400 --> 00:45:13.520
darkest, coldest winter anywhere.

00:45:13.520 --> 00:45:24.720
The sun sets in March at the South Pole and won't rise again for six months.

00:45:24.720 --> 00:45:31.120
For a few days at this time of year, high-altitude clouds of ice crystals continue to catch the

00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:52.000
sunlight even when the sun itself is far below the horizon.

00:45:52.000 --> 00:45:59.280
But soon, all trace of the sun disappears, and today's overwintering scientists remember

00:45:59.280 --> 00:46:04.880
the first explorers.

00:46:04.880 --> 00:46:10.880
These men, who endured the winter in flimsy wooden huts, borrowed knowledge from the Arctic

00:46:10.880 --> 00:46:18.840
pioneers before them, but they came here to study and explore rather than to hunt or exploit.

00:46:18.840 --> 00:46:28.480
They embodied the human spirit that has enabled us to survive at the poles.

00:46:28.480 --> 00:46:36.000
Here we're pushed to our limits, but in being pushed, humanity has achieved the extraordinary

00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:49.240
and opened up the last frontier.

00:46:49.240 --> 00:46:53.960
Next time, I'll be travelling to the ends of the Earth to witness for myself how rising

00:46:53.960 --> 00:47:03.760
temperatures are dramatically changing the frozen planet.

00:47:03.760 --> 00:47:08.860
I'll see how the animals are coping with their changing world and find out how these changes

00:47:08.860 --> 00:47:25.600
might even affect the whole planet.

00:47:25.600 --> 00:47:30.640
To tell the story of how we survive at the poles, frozen planet travelled to Siberia,

00:47:30.640 --> 00:47:37.880
the far north of Russia, where most of the Arctic's people live.

00:47:37.880 --> 00:47:43.920
The team got to know many extraordinary people.

00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:48.080
But one group of men above all really opened their eyes to what it means to live off the

00:47:48.080 --> 00:47:54.040
land in the most unforgiving environment on Earth.

00:47:54.040 --> 00:48:11.280
Men who take their life in their hands every day just to find their food.

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:17.880
Producer Dan Rees led the frozen planet team on their longest journey in the Arctic.

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:23.440
Their mission was to film walrus hunters in Russia's most remote region, Chukotka, the

00:48:23.440 --> 00:48:29.800
closest point to their old enemy, the United States, 4,000 miles and nine time zones east

00:48:29.800 --> 00:48:32.240
of Moscow.

00:48:32.240 --> 00:48:36.560
It's still a sensitive region, and the team soon learned that the border guards remain

00:48:36.560 --> 00:48:45.440
twitchy about foreigners with cameras.

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:50.600
To get permission to film, the crew relied on anthropologist Naomi Thompson, one of the

00:48:50.600 --> 00:48:56.160
few Westerners to have worked with the people here.

00:48:56.160 --> 00:49:00.720
But even Naomi finds a lot of doors closed to him.

00:49:00.720 --> 00:49:06.600
I have experienced a level of red tape I never could have anticipated.

00:49:06.600 --> 00:49:09.800
We've got our Russian visas in our passports.

00:49:09.800 --> 00:49:11.760
That's fine, but that's just the beginning.

00:49:11.760 --> 00:49:18.960
Here is the special permission given by the security services to be in the region of Chukotka.

00:49:18.960 --> 00:49:23.960
There's a special permission to be in every one of the population centers we will visit

00:49:23.960 --> 00:49:25.320
on our trip.

00:49:25.320 --> 00:49:29.540
Permission to shoot with a telephoto lens.

00:49:29.540 --> 00:49:34.860
Permission to shoot from a helicopter.

00:49:34.860 --> 00:49:38.680
Permission to shoot in a natural reserve.

00:49:38.680 --> 00:49:40.720
But we still don't have our migration cards.

00:49:40.720 --> 00:49:42.380
I'm trying to get them.

00:49:42.380 --> 00:49:46.080
And if we don't get them by the end of the day, we're going to be deported back to Alaska.

00:49:46.080 --> 00:49:48.560
It'll take three months to get new ones.

00:49:48.560 --> 00:49:53.480
Naomi's paper chase takes two days, but finally the team is allowed to begin their journey

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:55.280
to the hunters camp.

00:49:55.280 --> 00:50:00.160
With no roads, sometimes the only possible route is over the fast melting surfaces of

00:50:00.160 --> 00:50:07.640
the lakes.

00:50:07.640 --> 00:50:28.720
They need to be prepared for a quick exit.

00:50:28.720 --> 00:50:32.020
The next day, the crew finally reach the coast.

00:50:32.020 --> 00:50:38.280
The hunting camp is now just 30 miles away across a bay, but the crossing could be risky.

00:50:38.280 --> 00:50:41.240
The frozen sea is melting fast in the spring sunshine.

00:50:41.240 --> 00:50:45.440
I'm confident it's safe.

00:50:45.440 --> 00:50:50.600
It's not going to break underneath us.

00:50:50.600 --> 00:50:51.600
50-50.

00:50:51.600 --> 00:50:52.600
50-50.

00:50:52.600 --> 00:50:53.600
50-50.

00:50:53.600 --> 00:51:01.600
The locals employ several generations of Arctic transport from the ancient to the merely antique.

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:11.160
With the sea ice cracking up beneath them, Dan has to trust the crew and a ton of filming

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:13.880
gear to the experience of the locals.

00:51:13.880 --> 00:51:24.560
Even by their standards, this was fast becoming a risky journey.

00:51:24.560 --> 00:51:33.360
Where the first sleds cross just minutes before is now impossible.

00:51:33.360 --> 00:51:34.360
It's broken right up.

00:51:34.360 --> 00:51:38.400
We can't cross the leads in the ice here, so we need to get on some boats and the ice

00:51:38.400 --> 00:51:42.600
is just breaking up too fast for us at the moment.

00:51:42.600 --> 00:51:47.040
Fortunately the hunters from the camp turn up in the nick of time to rescue the crew

00:51:47.040 --> 00:51:59.840
for the final leg of the journey.

00:51:59.840 --> 00:52:05.240
Six days and 5,000 miles from home, the crew finally arrive at the place that will be their

00:52:05.240 --> 00:52:14.200
home for the next month.

00:52:14.200 --> 00:52:19.720
This is an active hunting camp and that evening the team are introduced to the realities of

00:52:19.720 --> 00:52:21.720
fending for yourself in the Arctic.

00:52:21.720 --> 00:52:30.720
We've just seen a walrus hunt out here in the waters of the Bering Sea.

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:31.720
All the meat will be eaten.

00:52:31.720 --> 00:52:32.720
They eat an awful lot of the internal organs as well.

00:52:32.720 --> 00:52:33.720
They use the skin, they use the sinews, they use the stomach for making drums, so it will

00:52:33.720 --> 00:52:34.720
get used very heavily and it is completely free range.

00:52:34.720 --> 00:52:49.240
Wild caught animal.

00:52:49.240 --> 00:52:54.240
You can't grow any vegetables up here, but sea mammal meat contains all the nutrients

00:52:54.240 --> 00:52:58.160
the hunters need and is very low in cholesterol.

00:52:58.160 --> 00:53:00.960
Dan is keen to try some of Collier's health food.

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:06.240
Well, this is yesterday's seal and a chunk of flesh there.

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:09.240
Behind it is some intestine.

00:53:09.240 --> 00:53:12.240
We should try a bit.

00:53:12.240 --> 00:53:15.240
That's seal intestine.

00:53:15.240 --> 00:53:19.640
It's quite fishy.

00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:20.640
Fishing rubber.

00:53:20.640 --> 00:53:21.640
That's not too bad.

00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:24.760
I thought it was going to be more disgusting than it actually is.

00:53:24.760 --> 00:53:25.760
The salt helps.

00:53:25.760 --> 00:53:26.760
Yeah.

00:53:26.760 --> 00:53:30.600
Would you like to try what we eat?

00:53:30.600 --> 00:53:31.600
You want a little bit of corn coma?

00:53:31.600 --> 00:53:32.600
No?

00:53:32.600 --> 00:53:33.600
I don't blame you.

00:53:33.600 --> 00:53:34.600
Yours is much better.

00:53:34.600 --> 00:53:48.880
As well as providing food, the animals here traditionally provided transport.

00:53:48.880 --> 00:53:55.280
Collier and Pasha, the oldest hunters, keep alive the skill of building walrus skin boats.

00:53:55.280 --> 00:54:01.080
You don't need a welding torch to repair this boat, just a juicy lump of seal fat to bung

00:54:01.080 --> 00:54:04.560
any holes.

00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:08.920
By living amongst them, the crew had really begun to get to know the hunters.

00:54:08.920 --> 00:54:16.000
But there was one big part of their lives which remained a mystery.

00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:22.340
As spring turns to summer, the hunters head out to an island to gather seabird eggs.

00:54:22.340 --> 00:54:29.320
The crew had heard stories of this but had little idea of what it was going to involve.

00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:30.320
That's where they climb.

00:54:30.320 --> 00:54:32.320
Those are impressive heights.

00:54:32.320 --> 00:54:36.320
It's quite something.

00:54:36.320 --> 00:54:43.440
For cameraman Ted Giffords, this was the first sight of the rock face he was about to work

00:54:43.440 --> 00:54:48.460
on.

00:54:48.460 --> 00:54:53.920
These cliffs can be treacherous as Collier, the expert egg collector, knows only too well.

00:54:53.920 --> 00:55:23.800
Ted will rely on steel stakes for anchors and

00:55:23.800 --> 00:55:27.800
an array of climbing gear and ropes.

00:55:27.800 --> 00:55:38.920
Collier preferred to stick with the simpler method that has served him well for many years.

00:55:38.920 --> 00:55:44.440
The crew can't quite believe what they're seeing.

00:55:44.440 --> 00:56:06.280
The limestone cliff is loose and crumbling because of hundreds of years of freezing and

00:56:06.280 --> 00:56:09.520
thawing.

00:56:09.520 --> 00:56:24.360
And for a highly trained professional climber like Ted, it's a dangerous descent.

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:29.760
Collier has been climbing here for four decades, but this is the first time he's had company

00:56:29.760 --> 00:56:36.060
and sometimes he forgets that Ted is just below him.

00:56:36.060 --> 00:56:40.240
Climbing supervisor Adam Scott holds his breath as he watches.

00:56:40.240 --> 00:56:55.360
A fall to the rocks 100 metres below would almost certainly be fatal.

00:56:55.360 --> 00:57:03.000
This is the most hardcore thing I've ever seen.

00:57:03.000 --> 00:57:06.800
It was when the team returned to the boat to finish filming that they got their most

00:57:06.800 --> 00:57:16.080
spectacular view of the lengths Collier was going to, to get his dinner.

00:57:16.080 --> 00:57:22.000
This shot sent the team home with a fresh perspective and a deep respect for these people

00:57:22.000 --> 00:57:24.800
who still live off the land in the Arctic.

00:57:24.800 --> 00:57:49.480
A way of life for which there is no safety net.

00:57:49.480 --> 00:58:04.080
Thank you.
